<Layout
  title="Irish tax questions answered: PAYE, USC and PRSI 2026"
  description="Straight answers on tax credits, emergency tax, the cut-off point, USC bands and PRSI, for anyone trying to work out why their payslip looks the way it does."
>
  <div class="prose prose-slate max-w-none">
    <h1>Frequently Asked Questions - Irish Salary & Tax</h1>

    <h2>Income Tax</h2>
    <details open>
      <summary><strong>What tax credits am I entitled to?</strong></summary>
      <p>Every PAYE employee receives the Personal Tax Credit (€2,000 single / €4,000 married) and the Employee PAYE Tax Credit (€2,000). Single parents get an additional €1,900 (SPCCC). Married couples with one income can claim €1,950 (Home Carer Credit). Other credits include Rent Tax Credit, medical expenses relief, and flat rate expenses for specific occupations.</p>
<MiniSimAutonome client:visible kind="takehome" lang="en" locale="en-IE" href="/" />

    </details>
    <details>
      <summary><strong>How does married assessment work?</strong></summary>
      <p>Married couples can choose joint assessment, which provides a combined SRCOP of up to €88,000 (€53,000 for one spouse plus up to €35,000 for the other) and double Personal Tax Credits (€4,000). The €53,000 band can be allocated to either spouse; the €35,000 increase is limited to the second income and cannot be transferred. This is most beneficial when one spouse earns significantly more than the other.</p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary><strong>What is the marginal rate of tax?</strong></summary>
      <p>The marginal rate is the tax rate applied to your next euro of income. Above the SRCOP it is 47.2% up to €70,044 (40% income tax + 3% USC + 4.2% PRSI) and 52.2% beyond, where USC rises to 8%. Below the SRCOP, the marginal rate depends on which USC band you are in, typically 20% + 3% USC + 4.2% PRSI = 27.2%. PRSI rises to 4.35% from 1 October 2026.</p>
    </details>

    <h2>USC</h2>
    <details>
      <summary><strong>Can I avoid paying USC?</strong></summary>
      <p>USC is payable on all income above €13,000. Unlike income tax, it cannot be reduced by tax credits. The only way to reduce USC is to reduce gross income - for example through pension contributions (though USC technically applies before pension deductions for occupational pensions). Medical card holders earning under €60,000 pay reduced USC rates.</p>
    </details>

    <h2>PRSI</h2>
    <details>
      <summary><strong>What benefits does PRSI provide?</strong></summary>
      <p>Class A PRSI contributions entitle you to: State Pension (Contributory) at 66, Jobseeker's Benefit (up to 9 months), Illness Benefit, Maternity/Paternity/Parent's Benefit, Invalidity Pension, and Treatment Benefit (dental, optical). The State Pension (Contributory) is currently €299.30 per week for a full contribution record.</p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary><strong>Is there a PRSI ceiling?</strong></summary>
      <p>No, there is no upper earnings limit for employee PRSI. The 4.2% rate, 4.35% from 1 October 2026, applies to all earnings once weekly pay exceeds €352. Unlike many European countries, Ireland does not cap social insurance contributions. That single fact is why the marginal rate stays above fifty-two percent all the way up the scale instead of falling back once a ceiling is passed, and why a bonus is taxed at exactly the same combined rate as the salary that earned it.</p>
    </details>

    <h2>Pension</h2>
    <details>
      <summary><strong>How much tax relief do I get on pension contributions?</strong></summary>
      <p>Pension contributions get tax relief at your marginal income tax rate (20% or 40%). A €100 contribution costs €60 net if you're paying the higher rate (40% relief). Age-related limits apply: 15% of earnings (under 30), rising to 40% (over 60), on a maximum of €115,000 earnings. Pension contributions do not reduce your USC or PRSI.</p>
    </details>

    <h2>Employment</h2>
    <details>
      <summary><strong>What is the minimum wage in Ireland?</strong></summary>
      <p>The national minimum wage for 2026 is €14.15 per hour for workers aged 20 and over. Reduced rates apply for younger workers: 80% for under-18s and 90% for 18-19 year olds. The Living Wage (recommended) is €14.80 per hour. Employers must also pay PRSI on top, and a worker on the minimum wage for a full year falls below the standard rate cut-off point, so none of that income is taxed at the higher rate.</p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary><strong>How much does an employer pay on top of my salary?</strong></summary>
      <p>Employers pay PRSI at 9% (for weekly earnings up to €552) or 11.25% (above €552). On a €50,000 salary, employer PRSI is approximately €5,644 over 2026, making the total employment cost about €55,644. Employers may also contribute to pension schemes and provide benefits (health insurance, company car) which have their own tax implications.</p>
    </details>
  </div>

